#!/bin/sh
# mission [N]  prints a mission brief, the same text as the panel on the page.
# Generated by guest-crypto/make-crypto.py: edit that file, not this one.
case "$1" in
  0) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 0: Warm-up   [optional, 50 bonus points, untimed]

Objective: Read the CTF orientation in the terminal with cat.

  cat orientation.txt

Get comfortable before the clock starts. Your home folder holds
orientation.txt: how a CTF works, what a flag looks like, scoring and
the crypto tools you will use.

cat prints a file on the screen. Click the terminal, type cat
orientation.txt and press Enter. The warm-up flag is at the bottom.

Submit it for 50 bonus points, or skip the warm-up. The competition
clock starts when you begin Mission 1.

When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}     Stuck? hint 0
EOF
  ;;
  1) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 1: Not so secret   [Easy, 100 points]

Objective: Decode a base64 message and learn why encoding is not encryption.

  cd ~/mission1
  cat message.b64

message.b64 looks scrambled, but it is only encoded with base64: a way
to write any data using letters, digits, +, / and =.

Encoding is not encryption. There is no key: anyone who knows the
encoding can turn it back.

Useful command: base64
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 1   (1 hint, 5 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  2) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 2: Hex marks the spot   [Easy, 100 points]

Objective: Turn hexadecimal back into readable text.

  cd ~/mission2
  cat secret.hex

secret.hex is a long run of digits and the letters a to f. That is
hexadecimal: every two characters are one byte, one letter of the
message.

xxd is a hex tool. It turns bytes into hex, and hex back into bytes.

Useful command: xxd
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 2   (1 hint, 5 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  3) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 3: Thirteen steps   [Easy, 100 points]

Objective: Undo ROT13 by translating letters with tr.

  cd ~/mission3
  cat message.txt

ROT13 moves every letter 13 places along the alphabet: A becomes N, B
becomes O, and so on. Do it twice and you are back where you started.

tr (translate) swaps characters: give it the letters to change and the
letters to change them into. Feed it the file with <.

Useful command: tr
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 3   (1 hint, 5 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  4) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 4: Hail Caesar   [Medium, 200 points]

Objective: Brute-force a Caesar cipher by trying every shift.

  cd ~/mission4
  cat message.txt

Julius Caesar hid messages by shifting every letter the same number of
places. This message was shifted, but nobody wrote down how far.

There are only 25 possible shifts. When the key is that small, try them
all: that is called brute force. The caesar tool can shift by one
number, or try them all.

Useful commands: caesar, grep
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 4   (2 hints, 10 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  5) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 5: Mirror, mirror   [Medium, 200 points]

Objective: Reverse the Atbash cipher with a flipped alphabet.

  cd ~/mission5
  cat mirror.txt

This message uses Atbash, one of the oldest known ciphers: the alphabet
is flipped, so A becomes Z, B becomes Y, C becomes X, and so on.

Flipping twice gives you back the original. You already know a tool that
swaps one set of letters for another.

Useful command: tr
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 5   (2 hints, 10 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  6) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 6: Dots and dashes   [Medium, 200 points]

Objective: Decode a Morse code signal and build the flag from it.

  cd ~/mission6
  cat README.txt

Morse code turns letters into short and long signals: . is short and -
is long. Letters are separated by spaces, words by /.

Morse has no curly braces, so README.txt tells you how to turn the
decoded code into a flag.

Useful command: morse
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 6   (2 hints, 10 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  7) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 7: Fingerprints   [Medium, 200 points]

Objective: Crack a SHA-256 hash by fingerprinting a list of guesses.

  cd ~/mission7
  cat target.sha256

A hash is a fingerprint of some text. The same text always gives the
same hash, but you cannot turn a hash back into the text.

candidates.txt holds 100 possible flags. Exactly one has the fingerprint
in target.sha256. Password crackers work the same way: hash every guess
from a list and compare.

Useful commands: hashlines, grep
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 7   (2 hints, 10 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  8) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 8: XOR marks   [Hard, 400 points]

Objective: Brute-force a single-byte XOR key.

  cd ~/mission8
  cat cipher.hex

XOR is a building block of most modern ciphers. Every byte of this
message was XORed with the same secret key byte: a number from 1 to 255.

One byte means only 255 possible keys. Same trick as Caesar: try every
key and look for the one that gives readable text.

Useful commands: xor, grep
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 8   (2 hints, 20 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  9) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 9: Keyed up   [Hard, 400 points]

Objective: Find the keyword, then decrypt a Vigenère cipher.

  cd ~/mission9
  ls

secret.txt is encrypted with the Vigenère cipher: each letter is shifted
by a different amount, set by a keyword that repeats. Brute force won't
work here: there are far too many keys.

But somebody left a briefing behind, scrambled with a cipher you already
know.

Useful commands: tr, vigenere
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 9   (2 hints, 20 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  10) cat <<'EOF'
Mission 10: The vault   [Very Hard, 800 points]

Objective: Crack the keyword's hash, then open the vault.

  cd ~/mission10
  cat README.txt

The final vault combines three skills. It was encrypted with a Vigenère
keyword, then base64-encoded. Nobody wrote the keyword down: only its
SHA-256 fingerprint survived.

Crack the fingerprint against the wordlist, then use the keyword to open
the vault.

Useful commands: hashlines, grep, base64, vigenere
When you have the flag: submit CYBA{...}
Stuck? hint 10   (4 hints, 40 points each: 5% of this mission)
EOF
  ;;
  *) cat <<'EOF'
Your missions:
   0  Warm-up (optional)           Bonus       50 pts  (bonus, untimed)
   1  Not so secret                Easy       100 pts
   2  Hex marks the spot           Easy       100 pts
   3  Thirteen steps               Easy       100 pts
   4  Hail Caesar                  Medium     200 pts
   5  Mirror, mirror               Medium     200 pts
   6  Dots and dashes              Medium     200 pts
   7  Fingerprints                 Medium     200 pts
   8  XOR marks                    Hard       400 pts
   9  Keyed up                     Hard       400 pts
  10  The vault                    Very Hard  800 pts
                                             2700 pts

Read one with: mission 1   (up to 10)
EOF
  ;;
esac
